WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.190
<v Narrator>Sales teams go home. Buyers keep calling.

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.673
<v Narrator>After hours, a buyer with a big order usually reaches voicemail,

3
00:00:10.753 --> 00:00:14.180
<v Narrator>and may call a competitor next. This agent picks up.

4
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.590
<v Narrator>Calls arrive on Contoso's sales number in Teams, through Teams Phone extensibility.

5
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.003
<v Narrator>After hours,

6
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:31.434
<v Narrator>Azure Communication Services Call Automation bridges the audio to Voice Live,

7
00:00:31.514 --> 00:00:33.860
<v Narrator>where GPT Realtime takes the details.

8
00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:37.691
<v Narrator>Your server scores the lead and decides what happens next:

9
00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:41.180
<v Narrator>a page to the on-call rep in Teams, or the morning queue.

10
00:00:43.604 --> 00:00:47.287
<v Narrator>7:30 on a Wednesday evening, and Contoso's sales team has gone home.

11
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:50.634
<v Narrator>Your server checks the clock, so the agent takes the call.

12
00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:54.210
<v Agent>Thanks for calling Contoso Industrial Supply.

13
00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.000
<v Agent>I'm an automated assistant.

14
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.507
<v Agent>Our sales team isn't available right now,

15
00:00:58.587 --> 00:01:02.394
<v Agent>but I can take your details and have the right person call you back.

16
00:01:02.474 --> 00:01:04.469
<v Agent>What are you looking for today?

17
00:01:05.795 --> 00:01:08.745
<v Dana>Hi, this is Dana Morgan from Northwind Traders.

18
00:01:08.825 --> 00:01:12.295
<v Dana>We need about 12,000 hex bolts by the end of the month.

19
00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.579
<v Agent>Thanks, Dana.

20
00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:16.326
<v Agent>Hex bolts are in our industrial fasteners line,

21
00:01:16.406 --> 00:01:18.773
<v Agent>and 12,000 meets the 5,000 minimum order.

22
00:01:18.853 --> 00:01:21.550
<v Agent>Will you be the one signing off on this order?

23
00:01:22.906 --> 00:01:25.128
<v Narrator>One sentence filled in most of the lead.

24
00:01:25.208 --> 00:01:28.725
<v Narrator>The server scores each answer against your rules, in plain English.

25
00:01:28.805 --> 00:01:30.676
<v Narrator>The model never sees the score.

26
00:01:31.484 --> 00:01:34.734
<v Dana>Yes, I'm the purchasing manager, so it's my call.

27
00:01:35.055 --> 00:01:38.448
<v Agent>I have your number as 425-555-0193.

28
00:01:38.528 --> 00:01:42.325
<v Agent>Is that the best number for a callback?

29
00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.584
<v Narrator>A decision maker: eight of eight.

30
00:01:45.664 --> 00:01:49.700
<v Narrator>But the server won't save the lead until Dana confirms the callback number.

31
00:01:50.516 --> 00:01:52.566
<v Dana>Yes, that's the best number.

32
00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:53.936
<v Agent>Thanks, Dana.

33
00:01:54.016 --> 00:01:56.284
<v Agent>Our sales team opens at 8 AM tomorrow,

34
00:01:56.364 --> 00:01:59.425
<v Agent>and someone will call you within an hour of opening.

35
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:01.392
<v Narrator>Saved.

36
00:02:01.472 --> 00:02:04.127
<v Narrator>The server picked the owner and the deadline,

37
00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:06.861
<v Narrator>and queued the follow-up for Teams and email.

38
00:02:06.941 --> 00:02:09.160
<v Narrator>Nobody was paged: hot isn't urgent.

39
00:02:11.215 --> 00:02:13.279
<v Narrator>Same server, 10:30 that morning.

40
00:02:13.359 --> 00:02:15.235
<v Narrator>Only the clock has changed.

41
00:02:17.627 --> 00:02:22.680
<v Narrator>Sales is open, so the server sends the call straight to the sales queue in Teams.

42
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.797
<v Narrator>No agent, no questions, and caller ID flagged as not verified.

43
00:02:28.876 --> 00:02:30.775
<v Narrator>Back to 7:30 in the evening.

44
00:02:30.855 --> 00:02:35.856
<v Narrator>Ben Ortiz is on call until 9, and your policy decides when he's worth interrupting.

45
00:02:36.862 --> 00:02:40.429
<v Sam>This is Sam Rivera, operations director at Tailwind Traders.

46
00:02:40.509 --> 00:02:44.774
<v Sam>Our packaging line is down and we need 2,000 cartons by tomorrow morning.

47
00:02:44.854 --> 00:02:46.892
<v Sam>Can I talk to someone tonight?

48
00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:48.246
<v Agent>Thanks, Sam.

49
00:02:48.326 --> 00:02:53.389
<v Agent>Cartons are in our packaging supplies line, and 2,000 meets the 1,000 minimum order.

50
00:02:53.469 --> 00:02:57.526
<v Agent>I'll check who's available as soon as I've confirmed your details.

51
00:02:57.606 --> 00:02:59.930
<v Agent>I have your number as 425-555-0162.

52
00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.638
<v Agent>Is that the best number for a callback?

53
00:03:03.994 --> 00:03:07.477
<v Narrator>Sam asked for a person, but the model can't promise one.

54
00:03:07.557 --> 00:03:12.344
<v Narrator>It only notes the request, and the server judges urgency from Sam's own words.

55
00:03:13.097 --> 00:03:17.014
<v Narrator>When Sam confirms the number, the server decides whether to page.

56
00:03:17.094 --> 00:03:18.737
<v Narrator>Watch the Teams panel.

57
00:03:19.576 --> 00:03:21.286
<v Sam>Yes, that's my cell.

58
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.050
<v Agent>Thanks, Sam. I'm paging our on-call sales rep now, so please stay on the line.

59
00:03:30.165 --> 00:03:34.662
<v Narrator>Ben picks up already knowing the score, the urgency and why he was paged.

60
00:03:34.742 --> 00:03:38.075
<v Narrator>That context travels with the call in VoIP headers.

61
00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:43.109
<v Narrator>Hot and urgent, inside on-call cover: that's your server's rule for a page,

62
00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:46.340
<v Narrator>and the reason is written into the lead's follow-up.

63
00:03:48.437 --> 00:03:52.796
<v Narrator>The same kind of call at 10:30 at night: a conveyor line is down,

64
00:03:52.876 --> 00:03:55.897
<v Narrator>and Robin Diaz needs lock nuts first thing.

65
00:03:55.977 --> 00:03:58.227
<v Narrator>But on-call cover ended at 9.

66
00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.050
<v Robin>Yes, that's the right number.

67
00:04:01.399 --> 00:04:05.237
<v Agent>There's no one on call right now, so I can't put you through to a person.

68
00:04:05.317 --> 00:04:06.301
<v Agent>Thanks, Robin.

69
00:04:06.381 --> 00:04:08.527
<v Agent>Our sales team opens at 8 AM tomorrow,

70
00:04:08.607 --> 00:04:11.509
<v Agent>and someone will call you within an hour of opening.

71
00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:15.827
<v Narrator>Hot and urgent, but outside on-call cover, so no page.

72
00:04:15.907 --> 00:04:18.537
<v Narrator>The server tells the agent what it can promise,

73
00:04:18.617 --> 00:04:21.032
<v Narrator>and writes the reason into the follow-up.

74
00:04:23.424 --> 00:04:27.030
<v Narrator>Every call is counted in the lead store: leads captured,

75
00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:30.854
<v Narrator>qualified and routed, live transfers, and pages answered.

76
00:04:32.513 --> 00:04:36.689
<v Narrator>Under the hood, the model holds the conversation and fills in the lead.

77
00:04:36.769 --> 00:04:40.163
<v Narrator>It never sees the score, and it can't promise a person.

78
00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:43.417
<v Narrator>Your server checks the hours, scores the lead,

79
00:04:43.497 --> 00:04:47.850
<v Narrator>insists on a confirmed number, picks the owner and decides whether to page.

80
00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.173
<v Narrator>Every decision lands in the trail.

81
00:04:52.373 --> 00:04:56.327
<v Narrator>Making it yours is mostly configuration: sales hours and on-call cover,

82
00:04:56.407 --> 00:05:00.083
<v Narrator>the scoring rules, your catalog's minimum orders, and your reps.

83
00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:01.954
<v Narrator>The spec, the tests

84
00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:06.313
<v Narrator>and this demo script give your coding agent the context for the rest.

85
00:05:08.913 --> 00:05:10.839
<v Narrator>After-hours sales lead capture.

86
00:05:10.919 --> 00:05:15.343
<v Narrator>Run it in simulation in two minutes, then connect it to your own Teams number.
